virglrenderer: Venus pipeline create infos stay inside what the driver indexes

What: each stage of a graphics or compute pipeline is one stage bit the
pipeline may have, once, with an entry name and code (a module, or SPIR-V
chained into the stage whose size is a whole number of words); its
specialization entries lie inside its data; dynamic states are ones Mesa's
vk_graphics_state knows; vertex bindings, locations, divisors and strides,
viewport and scissor counts, color blend attachment counts (equal to the
subpass's or the rendering info's colors where the attachments are read),
color write enables, attachment locations and input indices are within the
device's limits; the subpass is one the pass has; rendering info colors and
view mask within the limits; sample counts and sample locations ones the
device has; topology, cull mode, compare and stencil ops, blend factors and
ops, logic op, blend overlap and robustness behaviors are known values
(fields a dynamic state replaces are left alone); creation feedback has one
entry per stage or none. A refused create ends the app's context (the guest
creates pipelines without waiting).
Why: Mesa's vk_graphics_state and vk_pipeline (KosmicKrisp, NDEBUG) copy
viewports into arrays of 16 (32 viewports: SIGBUS), color blend state into 8
(32: stack overflow), vertex input into 32, sample locations into 32 and
attachment locations into 8, index per-stage arrays by the stage bit, read
specialization data at the entry's offset (a host read), write creation
feedback per entry, and end their switches in UNREACHABLE on an unknown
dynamic state, blend factor, op, logic op, compare op or cull mode; a stage
without a module dereferences NULL in both drivers; MoltenVK reads a blend
attachment per subpass color (3 against 1: SIGSEGV). Inline SPIR-V in a stage
skipped vkr's only shader check (codeSize % 4).
Upstream: OmacVM policy (spec VUIDs), draft only.
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-venus-commands.c (the p- cases).

